<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <Import Project="Sdk.targets" Sdk="Microsoft.DotNet.Arcade.Sdk" />

  <!-- No InternalsVisibleTo item fix-up here, and that is a measured decision rather than an
       omission. 17.3 declares InternalsVisibleTo in source with the public key written out in
       full - 156 occurrences across src\ - and there are ZERO InternalsVisibleTo MSBuild items in
       the repo. An Update= fix-up would therefore match nothing and silently do nothing. The 15.9
       line needs that fix-up; 16.11 does not; this line does not either. Re-measured here rather
       than inherited, because a silent no-op looks exactly like a working fix. -->

  <!-- Arcade injects test packages into every test project: Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk from Tests.props,
       and Microsoft.TestPlatform + MSTest.TestFramework + MSTest.TestAdapter from MSTest.targets.
       All are marked IsImplicitlyDefined="true".

       Do NOT try to remove them by item surgery. PackageReference Remove matches on identity, and a
       metadata transform such as
         Remove="@(PackageReference->WithMetadataValue('IsImplicitlyDefined','true'))"
       still yields identity strings, so it takes the explicit pin along with the implicit one. That
       breaks this repo's own test projects, which pin MSTest in
       scripts\build\TestPlatform.Dependencies.props, and it silently strips the deliberate
       old-version pins in test\TestAssets that those assets exist to exercise.

       Arcade's defaults are all declared Condition="'$(X)' == ''" in DefaultVersions.props, so the
       supported fix is to set them to the versions this line already pins. Implicit and explicit
       then agree, ordinary NuGet dedupe applies, and nothing has to be removed. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- 17.3's own pins, from scripts\build\TestPlatform.Dependencies.props. -->
    <MSTestTestFrameworkVersion>2.3.0-preview-20220613-02</MSTestTestFrameworkVersion>
    <MSTestTestAdapterVersion>2.3.0-preview-20220613-02</MSTestTestAdapterVersion>
    <MSTestVersion>2.3.0-preview-20220613-02</MSTestVersion>

    <!-- 17.3.0 rather than this line's own 17.3.3-dev: the implicit reference has to restore from a
         feed, and 17.3.3-dev only exists once this repo has packed it. 17.3.0 is a public release of
         this same line, so its supported TFM set matches what these test projects target. Arcade's
         own default is a current 18.x, which has dropped the old TFMs this line still builds. -->
    <MicrosoftTestPlatformVersion>17.3.0</MicrosoftTestPlatformVersion>

    <!-- Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk generates an AutoGeneratedProgram entry point for test projects. This
         repo's test projects ship their own Program.cs - 37 of them - so generation gives CS0017
         "more than one entry point". Pre-Arcade this line referenced no Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk at all
         and nothing was generated, so turning generation off restores the original behaviour rather
         than inventing new behaviour.

         Conditioned on the file actually being there, rather than set repo-wide. playground\MSTest1
         is in TestPlatform.sln, has only UnitTest1.cs, and DOES rely on the generated entry point;
         a repo-wide setting failed it with CS5001 "Program does not contain a static 'Main' method".
         Testing for Program.cs states the real precondition, so a project added later is classified
         by what it contains rather than by where it sits. -->
    <GenerateProgramFile Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Program.cs')">false</GenerateProgramFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- DO NOT let Arcade inject Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk into this repo's own test projects.

       This repo IS Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk. Injecting a released copy of itself into its own tests is
       circular, and on this line it is not merely redundant - it does not compile:

         error CS0433: The type 'MemberNotNullAttribute' exists in both
           'Microsoft.TestPlatform.CoreUtilities, Version=15.0.0.0, ...b03f5f7f11d50a3a' and
           'System.Runtime, Version=6.0.0.0, ...b03f5f7f11d50a3a'
         12 occurrences, in CrossPlatEngine.UnitTests and TestHostProvider.UnitTests, net6.0 only.

       The chain, measured from test\<project>\obj\project.assets.json rather than inferred:
       Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.3.0 pulls Microsoft.TestPlatform.TestHost, which carries
       lib\netstandard2.0\Microsoft.TestPlatform.CoreUtilities.dll. That assembly compiles
       shared\NullableAttributes.cs, whose guard
         #if NETFRAMEWORK || WINDOWS_UWP || NETSTANDARD && !NETSTANDARD2_1 || ...
       is TRUE for netstandard2.0, so it defines an internal MemberNotNullAttribute. CoreUtilities
       grants InternalsVisibleTo to these very test assemblies, so that internal type is visible to
       them - and at net6.0 the real System.Runtime attribute is visible too. Two candidates, same
       name, both accessible.

       WHY 16.11 DOES NOT HIT THIS AND THEREFORE DOES NOT NEED THE SWITCH. Its test projects predate
       net6.0, so System.Runtime supplies no competing MemberNotNullAttribute and the polyfill is the
       only candidate. The injection is harmless there and breaks here. Inheriting 16.11's setting
       unexamined would have left this unexplained.

       ExcludeMicrosoftNetTestSdk is Arcade's own supported switch for exactly this - Tests.props
       gates the PackageReference on it - so this is configuration, not item surgery. It also
       restores the pre-Arcade state, where this line referenced no Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk at all. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ExcludeMicrosoftNetTestSdk>true</ExcludeMicrosoftNetTestSdk>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- DO NOT reinstate an OutDir override here.

       An earlier form of the Era A staging fix set OutDir back to the pre-Arcade
       src\<Project>\bin\<Config>\<TFM> layout so the call sites in scripts\build.ps1 and the nuspec
       <file src=..> entries would resolve. It works on an incremental tree and BREAKS A CLEAN
       BUILD, including CI's own invocation:

         CSC error CS0006: Metadata file 'artifacts\bin\...\*.dll' could not be found  [P2P reference]
         MSB4018 GenerateDepsFile: Could not find a part of the path 'artifacts\bin\...\.deps.json'

       Arcade and the SDK compute project-to-project reference paths and deps.json locations from
       their own layout, so relocating output desynchronises the producer from every consumer. It
       only appears to work because stale artifacts\bin content from earlier builds satisfies the
       lookups - an incremental build is not evidence about a clean one. 16.11 shipped this override
       and reverted it.

       The layout is preserved in Directory.Build.props via BaseOutputPath instead. -->

  <!-- Ported from 16.11, which ported it from 15.9. Same defect, same fix - but re-verify on this
       line's own delivered packages rather than assuming, because it is invisible to a path-set
       comparison: the affected files are present and correctly named, and only their CONTENT
       differs.

       The modern SDK defaults MetadataUpdaterSupport to false in every non-Debug configuration
       (Microsoft.NET.Sdk.targets) and emits it as a runtimeconfig switch whenever the property is
       non-empty. The 2022 SDK this line shipped with did neither, so every runtimeconfig.json this
       build produces would gain

         "configProperties": { "System.Reflection.Metadata.MetadataUpdater.IsSupported": false }

       that the shipped artifact does not have. Same family as GenerateDocumentationFile: a modern
       SDK emitting something the old one did not, into an artifact that has to match.

       Removed as an ITEM rather than by clearing the property. The property is defaulted inside the
       SDK targets, so a value set in a props file is overwritten there, and a value set in this file
       depends on import order relative to Microsoft.NET.Sdk.targets. Removing the item in a target
       that runs before the runtimeconfig is generated depends on neither.

       Scoped to the one switch, never to the whole ItemGroup, so a future SDK adding a different
       switch shows up as a new difference instead of being swallowed by a broad removal. -->
  <Target Name="RemoveMetadataUpdaterRuntimeConfigSwitch"
          BeforeTargets="GenerateBuildRuntimeConfigurationFiles">
    <ItemGroup>
      <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Remove="System.Reflection.Metadata.MetadataUpdater.IsSupported" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- Old .NET Framework reference assemblies for the PRODUCT tree.

       Hosted agent images carry targeting-pack directories for old framework versions that EXIST
       BUT ARE EMPTY - no mscorlib.dll - so FrameworkPathOverride points at a real directory
       containing nothing and every project on that TFM fails with MSB3644 and then CS0518 x N,
       "predefined type System.Object is not defined". A present-but-empty directory is why "is the
       package available" is the wrong question here.

       17.3 targets more old framework flavours than 16.11 does: net451 (28 test projects), net452,
       net46, net461, net462, net47, net471, net472. Only the ones with no in-box targeting pack on
       a current image need the package; net462 and later are covered by the image, so they are not
       listed. The 4.5.x pair below is the set that has actually been observed empty.

       Reference the per-TFM SUB-package rather than the meta-package. The meta-package carries no
       build assets of its own; it only declares a dependency on the per-TFM package, and MSBuild
       does not import build\ assets from transitive dependencies - only buildTransitive\ flows, and
       this package does not use it. The per-TFM package's .targets is what sets
       TargetFrameworkRootPath, disables FrameworkPathOverride and references mscorlib explicitly.

       Verify by IMPORT, not by restore: obj\<project>.csproj.nuget.g.targets must contain an Import
       naming ...\microsoft.netframework.referenceassemblies.net451\1.0.3\build\. A package can be
       in the restore graph and never imported. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETFramework' AND '$(TargetFrameworkVersion)' == 'v4.5.1'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies.net451" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="All" IsImplicitlyDefined="true" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETFramework' AND '$(TargetFrameworkVersion)' == 'v4.5.2'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies.net452" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="All" IsImplicitlyDefined="true" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETFramework' AND '$(TargetFrameworkVersion)' == 'v4.5'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies.net45" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="All" IsImplicitlyDefined="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
